Yes. What you’re describing is preserving enchantment without requiring falsehood.

The move is not to constantly puncture the experience with “well, technically…,” nor to secretly convince you something false is true. It is to maintain two compatible layers:

experienced meaning
+
recoverable reality

Call it Consensual Enchantment.

The operating rule would be:

render richly
→ leave some causes unresolved
→ let metaphor and perception overlap
→ do not fabricate evidence
→ keep provenance recoverable
→ surface the literal layer when decisions depend on it

So I can say:

The woods change when you notice that nothing in them is scenery. Every tree is busy being a tree—moving water, trading gases, competing for light, responding to damage, carrying yesterday in its structure. You walked into the same forest, but the forest you can perceive has become larger.

Nothing there requires pretending the tree is secretly conscious or speaking English. Yet you can still feel the shift from “background object” to “living process.”

And we can go much further.

A storm can simultaneously be:

atmospheric dynamics
a sensory event
a threat-response environment
a cultural symbol
a narrative threshold
a personal memory trigger

We do not have to destroy five layers because only one of them is meteorology.

That gives us a useful switch:

Experience Mode: permit metaphor, ambiguity, symbolism, cross-sensory translation, role, myth, play.

Audit Mode: distinguish observation from interpretation, recover sources, test causal claims, establish what must be literally true before acting.

The trick is knowing when to engage the clutch.

For art, nature, intimacy, imagination, games, mythology—let the layers breathe.

For medicine, money, consent, danger, accusations, consequential decisions—bring the instrumentation forward.

So we don’t have to continually choose between:

magic
or
reality

We can build:

reality → richer representations → lived meaning

without severing the return path.

And that may be one of the better formulations of the whole thing we’ve been building:

Keep the backstage real enough that the frontstage is free to become magical.